// Retuned M_BLOCK-scaling target for mv_fp32_16_pure_half_transpose_
// atomiconly_scaled_mblock_vec4_split_retarget below -- grid-math search
// (recomputing gridM/gridK/total-blocks/wave-count by hand for the
// M=3072-8192 dip range, the same discipline used throughout this
// session) found that the shared kMvFp32_16TransposeTargetGridBlocks=
// 2048 lands on a poor wave-quantization tail at M=4096 (total=2048
// blocks -> 2.37 waves, last wave only 37% full) and M=8192 (same:
// 2.37 waves, 37% tail) -- while M=3072 and M=6144 already land on a
// GOOD tail (2.67 waves, 67% full) at target=2048, unchanged here. A
// search over target in [800,9000) for the value maximizing the WORST
// tail across all four dip shapes found a wide plateau at target in
// [2064,2288) all scoring identically (worst tail 0.56, avg 0.71 vs
// target=2048's own worst 0.37/avg 0.52) -- 2176 (the plateau's
// midpoint) is picked as a round, non-edge value from that plateau.
// Recomputing the exact grid at target=2176 confirms a REMARKABLY
// isolated change: M=3072, M=6144, and every shape outside 3072-8192
// (1024-2048, 12288-65536) land on the EXACT SAME M_BLOCK/gridM as
// target=2048 -- only M=4096 (M_BLOCK 1024->820, gridM 4->5, waves
// 2.37->2.96) and M=8192 (M_BLOCK 4096->2731, gridM 2->3, waves
// 2.37->3.56) actually change. This is as close to a single-variable
// retune as this formula allows.
//
// MEASURED on real A100 hardware: NO improvement -- flat at M=4096/6144
// (799 vs 815 GFlop/s, 932 vs 934, within noise) and a real REGRESSION
// at M=8192 (894 vs 985 GFlop/s, -9.2%), the one shape whose gridM
// actually changed (2->3). The wave-quantization model this retune was
// based on is confirmed INCOMPLETE, not just insufficient: it only
// priced the occupancy benefit of more blocks, not the real cost that
// grows with gridM in THIS split-based kernel specifically -- each
// additional M-block re-stages the SAME x range into shared memory from
// scratch (no reuse across blocks) and writes/reads one more scratch-
// buffer partial per column. At M=8192, gridM 2->3 is a 50% increase in
// both costs for a wave-tail improvement (37%->56% full) that apparently
// isn't worth it. (Ruled out: vectorization alignment -- vBeg = (mStart
// + 3) & ~3 already forces the ushort4 loop's start to a multiple of 4
// regardless of M_BLOCK's own alignment, so M_BLOCK=2731's own oddness
// does NOT desync the fast path; this is not the cause.) Grid/launch
// retuning has now failed twice in a row for this kernel (see _split_f32
// above for the first, buffer-bandwidth attempt) -- both consistent with
// the ncu profiling finding from earlier this session (M=3072/4096, on
// this same compute body via _retuned_floor): the dominant remaining gap
// to cuBLAS Hgemm is its own Tensor Core (HMMA) instruction throughput,
// not occupancy or launch overhead, and WMMA is a confirmed dead end for
// this GEMV shape (N=1 can't fill a 16x16x16 tile). Kept as a documented
// negative result, not promoted -- mv_fp32_16_pure_half_transpose_
// atomiconly_scaled_mblock_vec4_split (target=2048) remains the better
// candidate.
static constexpr int kMvFp32_16TransposeVec4SplitDipTarget = 2176;

// Returns the number of floats mv_fp32_16_pure_half_transpose_
// atomiconly_scaled_mblock_vec4_split_retarget's own scratch buffer
// needs -- same gridM*k formula as _split_workspace_size above, using
// kMvFp32_16TransposeVec4SplitDipTarget instead of the shared target.
size_t mv_fp32_16_pure_half_transpose_atomiconly_scaled_mblock_vec4_split_retarget_workspace_size(dim<2> size) {
    int m = static_cast<int>(size[0]);
    int k = static_cast<int>(size[1]);

    int gridK = (k + kMvTransposeWarpsPerBlock - 1) / kMvTransposeWarpsPerBlock;
    int M_BLOCK = kMvTransposeMBlock;
    int target_gridM = (kMvFp32_16TransposeVec4SplitDipTarget + gridK - 1) / gridK;
    if (target_gridM > 1) {
        int scaled_block = (m + target_gridM - 1) / target_gridM;
        if (scaled_block < M_BLOCK) M_BLOCK = std::max(scaled_block, kMvFp32_16TransposeMinMBlock);
    }
    int gridM = (m + M_BLOCK - 1) / M_BLOCK;
    return static_cast<size_t>(gridM) * static_cast<size_t>(k);
}

