// SECOND M_BLOCK-scaling target for the same M=3072-24576 dip -- tests
// the OPPOSITE hypothesis of kMvFp32_16TransposeVec4SplitDipTarget
// above. That first retune INCREASED gridM at M=8192 (target-driven
// wave-tail optimization, 2->3 blocks/column) and regressed -9.2% --
// diagnosed as the split design's own redundant cost that grows with
// gridM (each extra M-block re-stages the SAME x range into shared
// memory from scratch, and writes/reads one more scratch-buffer
// partial) outweighing the occupancy gain from a better wave tail. If
// that diagnosis is right, the opposite move -- REDUCING gridM at the
// shapes where it's currently high, even at some cost to wave-tail
// alignment -- should help instead of hurt. Grid-math search (widened
// this time to cover the full M=3072-24576 dip the user pointed out,
// not just 3072-8192) for a target where gridM <= the shared target=
// 2048's own value at EVERY shape in that dip (never increasing block
// count, only ever decreasing it) found a plateau at target in roughly
// [1024,1792]; 1280 (a round value from that plateau) is picked.
// Recomputed grid at target=1280: gridM drops from 6->4 at M=3072,
// 4->3 at M=4096, 3->2 at M=6144 (25-33% less redundant work at each,
// with wave-tail ALSO improving at all three: 67%->78%, 37%->78%,
// 67%->78%) -- M=8192 and every shape at or above it (12288-65536) land
// on the EXACT SAME grid as target=2048, completely untouched, so this
// cannot repeat the M=8192 regression. The one side effect outside the
// originally-targeted range: M=2048 also picks up a lower gridM (8->5,
// tail 37%->48%) since target=1280 crosses its own target_gridM
// threshold -- M=2048's own ratio to cuBLAS was already decent (0.92x)
// before this change, so this is a real, if secondary, thing to watch
// in the measurement, not just the 3072-24576 range this was aimed at.
static constexpr int kMvFp32_16TransposeVec4SplitFewerBlocksTarget = 1280;

